# Obsidian conventions

How this vault is organized and how to work in it: where notes and tasks live, how they are tagged
and linked, how to search, and how to use Obsidian from the terminal. It applies to every project in
the vault. Skills and agents read "The short version" before they write notes.

## The short version

1. Every task is `<project>/tasks/<TICKET-ID>/README.md`. Never file a task inside the topic it is
   about. With no ticket yet, the folder is a kebab-case slug.
2. A note says what it is about with **topic tags**. A topic tag is a folder path under the project's
   **topics root** (`docs/` unless the profile says otherwise), lowercased: `billing/invoices`.
   Tag only folders that exist; a cross-cutting note carries no topic tag.
3. Every note has a unique **alias**; a task's first alias is its folder name. Aliases are for
   finding a note, not for linking to it.
4. **Links carry a path**: `[[<vault path or unique file name>|<alias>]]`, and the full vault path
   for every `README.md`. A bare `[[Alias]]` does not resolve.
5. Folder `README.md` files are **hubs** that link down to their notes and up to their parent.
   Every other note starts with `> Up: [[<path>|<Hub alias>]]`.
6. **Move and rename with `second-brain ob move` / `second-brain ob rename`**, never `mv`.
7. **Frontmatter is the truth.** A task carries `title`, `status`, `next` (the next action, in one
   phrase), `branch` and `epic`. `status` is `planned`, `in-progress`, `in-review`, `done` or
   `abandoned`; its tag is `status/done` for the last two and `status/active` for the rest. Change
   both with `second-brain task set <ID> status=<status>`.
8. **Quote a YAML value** that holds a colon then a space or a `#`, starts with `-` or `[`, is a
   lone dash, or looks like a date or a number: `- "Runbook: rotate the API keys"`, `epic: "-"`.
9. A **sprint** is `<project>/sprints/<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`, named by its end date. A task joins it with
   the property `sprint: "<id>"`, always quoted. No `sprint` key means backlog.
10. Find things with the **Obsidian CLI** for metadata and **grep** for text. Scope every search.
11. After editing links, `second-brain ob unresolved verbose` must not list a target you wrote.

## Note metadata

Every substantive note carries frontmatter. Properties feed Bases; tags feed the tag pane, search
and the graph.

```yaml
---
aliases:
  - EX-123                 # a unique handle: the ticket id, a hub name or a short title
title: "Invoice totals are rounded twice"
status: in-progress        # tasks only
next: "Confirm the fix in staging"
sprint: "2026-10-14"       # tasks only; omit for backlog
type: task                 # the kind of note
tags:
  - billing/invoices       # topic tags: zero, one or several
  - type/task              # mirrors type
  - status/active          # coarse rollup of status
---
```

- **`aliases`** - at least one, unique across the vault. Most notes are named `README.md`, so the
  alias is the only name that tells them apart.
- **`type`** and the `type/<type>` tag - one of `task`, `bug`, `investigation`, `architecture`,
  `runbook`, `validation`, `tool`, `ops`, `onboarding`, `data-source`, `data-output`, `sprint`,
  `profile`, `moc`. A project may add its own. `moc` is for hubs.
- **`status`** - tasks use `planned`, `in-progress`, `in-review`, `done`, `abandoned`. The tag is
  the rollup: `status/done` for `done` and `abandoned`, `status/active` for the rest.
- **Task notes** also carry `title`, `next`, `branch`, `epic` and `carried_from` (the sprint ids a
  task was carried over from). The ticket id is the folder name and the first alias, not a property.

YAML traps. Quote a value when:

- it contains a colon then a space: `- "Runbook: rotate the API keys"` (else it is read as a key)
- it is a lone dash: `epic: "-"` (else it is read as empty)
- it is a sprint id, or any string shaped like a date or a number: `sprint: "2026-10-14"` (else it
  may be read as a date and miss the board filter)
- it contains `#`, or starts with `-` or `[`

## Topic tags

A **topic** is a folder under the project's topics root. Its **tag** is that folder's path below the
topics root, lowercased, with spaces and underscores written as hyphens:

```
folder  <project>/docs/billing/Invoices/   ->  tag  billing/invoices
folder  <project>/docs/api/rate_limits/    ->  tag  api/rate-limits
```

- Use the **deepest folder** the note is really about. A sub-topic implies its parent; do not add both.
- **Several topics, several tags. Cross-cutting, no tag.** Do not invent a catch-all such as `general`.
- **Only tag folders that exist.** Organizational folders (`tasks/`, `sprints/`) are not topics.
- **Tag to folder:** read the tag as a path under the topics root, matching case-insensitively and
  treating `_` and spaces as `-`. That folder's `README.md` is the topic's hub. With
  `topics_root: "."` in the profile, topic folders sit at the project root.

## Tasks

```
<project>/tasks/<TICKET-ID>/README.md      # the task
<project>/tasks/<TICKET-ID>/queries/ ...   # its queries, notebooks, decks and logs, beside it
<project>/tasks/README.md                  # the tasks hub
<project>/tasks/tasks.base                 # the task board
```

- One folder per task, directly under `tasks/`; its name is the README's first alias.
- No ticket yet: the folder is a kebab-case slug. Once the ticket exists, add the id as an alias
  with `second-brain task alias <slug> add <ID>`; every command then accepts the id. The folder
  keeps the slug: renaming a task folder is not supported yet.
- A goal tracked as one ticket per environment is **one** folder and one README, under the first
  environment's id, with every id in `aliases` and an `envs:` mapping in the frontmatter.
- The **task board** (`tasks.base`, Obsidian Bases) is a live table over the README frontmatter,
  with the views `Active`, `Current sprint`, `By topic`, `Needs a status` and `All`. Nothing is
  added to it by hand. A status edited in the board changes the property only; fix the `status/`
  tag the next time the note is touched.
- A status note is one line, newest first, under `## Status notes`:
  `` **YYYY-MM-DD (session `<id>`) - <text>** ``.

## Sprints

- One note per sprint, named by its end date, which is its id. The profile holds the cadence
  (`sprint_length_days`, `sprint_anchor_end`) and may name another folder than `sprints/`.
- Carrying a task over appends the old id to `carried_from` and sets `sprint` to the next id.
- One sprint is in progress at a time. Its task list is the board's `Current sprint` view; nothing
  is typed into the sprint note until it closes.
- A project whose profile says `uses_sprints: false` has no sprints: no sprint notes, no `sprint`
  on its tasks, no `Current sprint` view. Its tasks are tracked by status alone.

## Hubs and links

- **Every folder `README.md` is a hub** (`type: moc`). It links down to its notes and up to its
  parent hub. Every other note starts with an up-link line: `> Up: [[<path>|<Hub alias>]]`.
- **Links resolve by file name or path, never by alias.** Write a link the way Obsidian generates
  it, with the alias as display text:
  - the full vault path when the file name is not unique, which is every `README.md`:
    `[[clients/acme/projects/example/tasks/EX-123/README|EX-123]]`
  - the bare file name when it is unique in the vault: `[[obsidian-conventions|Obsidian Conventions]]`
  - `\|` instead of `|` when the link sits in a table cell
- Get the path from the alias: `obsidian aliases verbose | grep "^<alias>"`.
- **Check what you wrote:** `obsidian unresolved verbose` lists links that go nowhere.

## Moving and renaming

Use `obsidian move path="<vault path>" to="<existing folder or new path>"` and
`obsidian rename path="<vault path>" name="<new name>"`. Obsidian then rewrites every link to the
note. Create the destination folder first. Do not use `mv`: links are left to fix by hand.

## Obsidian CLI

Obsidian's own index is reachable from the terminal with `obsidian <command>`, while the app is
running. `second-brain ob <command>` finds the binary and stops early when the app is closed.
`obsidian help` lists everything.

| Need | Command |
|---|---|
| A note by id or alias | `obsidian aliases verbose \| grep -i "^<alias>"` |
| Notes carrying one exact tag | `obsidian tag name=billing/invoices verbose` |
| Every tag in use, with counts | `obsidian tags counts` |
| A project's task board | `obsidian base:query path="<project>/tasks/tasks.base" view=Active format=md` |
| What links to a note | `obsidian backlinks path="<vault path>"` |
| Links that go nowhere | `obsidian unresolved verbose` |
| Read or set a property | `obsidian property:read name=status path="<vault path>"`, `property:set name=status value=done path=...` |
| Headings of a long note | `obsidian outline path="<vault path>"` |
| Text inside one folder | `obsidian search:context query="<text>" path="<folder>"` |
| Move or rename a note | `obsidian move ...`, `obsidian rename ...` |

`obsidian tag name=<tag>` matches that exact tag, not its sub-tags. After many files change on disk
at once, the index can lag; `obsidian reload` forces a re-read.
A search that matches hundreds of notes can come back empty: keep queries selective and scoped with
`path=`. `second-brain search` falls back to grep when the CLI returns nothing.

